<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Measuring the metacognition of AI</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Richard Servajean
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:rservajean@yahoo.fr">rservajean@yahoo.fr</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Center for Brain Science, RIKEN, Saitama, Japan
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Philippe Servajean
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:philippe_gi@hotmail.fr">philippe_gi@hotmail.fr</a>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Psychology, Paul-Valéry University, Montpellier, France
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A robust decision-making process must take into account uncertainty, especially when the choice involves inherent risks. Because artificial Intelligence (AI) systems are increasingly integrated into decision-making workflows, managing uncertainty relies more and more on the metacognitive capabilities of these systems; i.e, their ability to assess the reliability of and regulate their own decisions. Hence, it is crucial to employ robust methods to measure the metacognitive abilities of AI. This paper is primarily a methodological contribution arguing for the adoption of the meta-<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> framework, or its model-free alternatives, as the gold standard for assessing the <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">metacognitive sensitivity</span> of AIs—the ability to generate confidence ratings that distinguish correct from incorrect responses. Moreover, we propose to leverage <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">signal detection theory</span> (SDT) to measure the ability of AIs to spontaneously regulate their decisions based on uncertainty and risk. To demonstrate the practical utility of these psychophysical frameworks, we conduct two series of experiments on three large language models (LLMs)—GPT-5, DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp, and Mistral-Medium-2508. In the first experiments, LLMs performed a primary judgment followed by a confidence rating. In the second, LLMs only performed the primary judgment, while we manipulated the risk associated with either response. On the one hand, applying the meta-<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> framework allows us to conduct comparisons along three axes: comparing an LLM to optimality, comparing different LLMs on a given task, and comparing the same LLM across different tasks. On the other hand, SDT allows us to assess whether LLMs become more conservative when risks are high.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">keywords</h6>artificial intelligence, large language models, metacognition, metacognitive efficiency, metacognitive sensitivity, decision-making, uncertainty, signal detection theory
</div><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_equal-contributors"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">equal-contributors: </span>These authors contributed equally to this work.</span></span></span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_equal-contributors"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">equal-contributors: </span>These authors contributed equally to this work.</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is widely acknowledged that artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming our societies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Makridakis (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stone et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gruetzemacher and Whittlestone (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie and Avila (2025)</a></cite>, in fields as diverse as education <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hwang and Chang (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yigci et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Memarian and Doleck (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stadler et al. (2024)</a></cite> and protein structure prediction and design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jumper et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Evans et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Winnifrith et al. (2023)</a></cite>. In particular, since the release of GPT-3.5 in 2022 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2022)</a></cite>, the massive adoption of conversational chatbots based on large language models (LLMs) by the public in their daily lives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Topics (2025)</a></cite> has reignited debates about the opportunities and risks posed by AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weidinger et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rillig et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jalil et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Massey et al. (2023)</a></cite>. This has motivated many researchers to test the current capabilities of AI systems across a broad range of tasks, and specifically those of conversational chatbots (see, e.g., Refs. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Johnson et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sumbal et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geerling et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Borges et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wójcik et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fijačko et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">de Winter (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Al-Ashwal et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abbas et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saab et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gencer and Aydin (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mbakwe et al. (2023)</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As AI systems are increasingly deployed across society, we identify three ways in which they can be incorporated into a decision process:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI decision-making:</span> The AI system operates alone without any human intervention. An example of this would be autonomous driving at Level 4 or 5 (as defined by the SAE J3016 standard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Committee (2021)</a></cite>), denoting systems that require no human oversight.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human-AI joint decision-making:</span> the AI system and humans take a decision as co-agents. One example is a setting in which the final decision results from a combination of human and AI judgments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steyvers et al. (2022)</a></cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AI-assisted decision-making:</span> the AI system makes recommendations that are ultimately validated by a human. An example of this would be AI-powered medical diagnostics, where the system flags potential abnormalities in scans and provides a risk score that a radiologist uses to reach a final medical conclusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Castilla et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that these categories only serve as primary landmarks along a broader continuum, reflecting the vast diversity of possible human-AI interaction patterns. See Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gomez et al. (2025)</a></cite> for a review of human-AI interaction patterns identified in the literature. Regardless, an effective decision-making process requires taking <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uncertainty</span> into account. Here, uncertainty concerns the probability that a judgment or a prediction (e.g., regarding the outcome of a choice) is correct. When uncertainty and risks are high, the most effective strategy is often to suspend judgment and prioritize further data collection before making the final decision (see the <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">exploration-exploitation dilemma</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berger-Tal et al. (2014)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mehlhorn et al. (2015)</a></cite>). Furthermore, in some decision-making contexts, the available options may differ considerably in the magnitude of their associated risks and/or benefits. In such cases, accounting for uncertainty becomes critically important. For instance, even a marginal amount of uncertainty regarding a catastrophic outcome can be enough to warrant an alternative choice that is more uncertain but offers lower risks in the failure scenario. Finally, in some multi-agent (human-human, human-AI or AI-AI) joint decision-making, quantifying the uncertainty of each agent’s prediction provides a principled way to arbitrate between divergent forecasts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bahrami et al. (2010)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steyvers et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhattacharyya et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koriat (2012)</a></cite>. Overall, note that researchers from fields as diverse as game theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Neumann and Morgenstern (2007)</a></cite>, computational neuroscience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parr et al. (2022)</a></cite> and machine learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vo et al. (2019)</a></cite> have investigated the question of finding optimal strategies within uncertain contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, managing uncertainty poses unique challenges, depending on how AI systems are incorporated into decision processes. In the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AI decision-making</span> scenario, AI systems operate autonomously. The challenges therefore concern the ability of AI systems to estimate uncertainty and adjust their decisions accordingly. Here, the role of humans is limited to providing preliminary information, such as acceptable risk thresholds. In contrast, <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">human-AI joint</span> and <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AI-assisted</span> decision-making can involve an additional challenge: the effective communication of uncertainty information between humans and AIs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steyvers et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steyvers et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Steyvers (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steyvers and Peters (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In these collaborative scenarios, AI systems must not only evaluate uncertainty, but also convey it transparently to humans (ideally accompanied by explanations about the sources of that uncertainty in order to determine whether it can be reduced, and, if so, how). For example, this could allow humans to strategically allocate verification efforts only to instances where the AI system identifies—and reports—its outputs as unreliable.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          This example constitutes a special case of the exploration-exploitation dilemma where the decision process involves both humans and AI.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Against this backdrop, the current work examines these uncertainty-related challenges by focusing specifically on LLMs, whose integration into decision-making workflows has become increasingly prevalent. More precisely, we investigate two specific capabilities of LLMs. First, the ability of LLMs to assess and report the reliability of their own predictions or judgments. Second, the ability of LLMs to spontaneously adjust their decisions based on estimated uncertainty and risk configuration. Note that AI’s ability to assess the reliability of its own outcomes is involved in all the aforementioned decision-making scenarios (i.e. AI decision-making, human-AI joint decision-making, and AI-assisted decision-making). This is also the case for the ability to report this confidence estimation except in the AI decision-making scenario where the AI operates autonomously. Arguably, the ability to adjust their decisions based on uncertainty and risk would be mainly involved in the AI decision-making scenario.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Two core dimensions underlie the ability of LLMs to assess and report the reliability of their own outcomes: <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">metacognitive sensitivity</span> and <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">metacognitive calibration</span> (also known as type 2 sensitivity and type 2 bias, respectively). The concepts of metacognitive sensitivity and metacognitive calibration originate from the fields of psychology and neuroscience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fleming (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maniscalco and Lau (2014)</a></cite>. They are typically applied in settings where a participant makes a primary judgment followed by a confidence rating—an assessment of the probability that the initial judgment was correct. Metacognitive sensitivity then refers to the individual’s ability to distinguish correct from incorrect responses through confidence ratings. On the other hand, metacognitive calibration refers to the extent to which these confidence ratings align with objective accuracy. For example, a confidence rating of 80% with respect to a set of predictions is considered to be well calibrated if 80% of those predictions are indeed correct. Importantly, one can certainly possess high metacognitive sensitivity while simultaneously exhibiting poor metacognitive calibration (or conversely). For instance, a participant might <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">systematically</span> assign a confidence rating of 100% to correct responses and a confidence rating of 99% to incorrect ones. In such a case, metacognitive sensitivity would be perfect, as confidence ratings perfectly distinguish correct responses from incorrect ones. However, metacognitive calibration would be poor, as the participant is massively overconfident (systematically assigning a confidence level of 99% to incorrect responses). Finally, it is important to stress that both dimensions are vital. Indeed, consider a scenario where an LLM communicates a confidence score regarding its own output. In this context, poor metacognitive calibration would cause the user to misinterpret the confidence score, whereas zero metacognitive sensitivity renders the confidence score entirely uninformative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Regardless, in what follows, our focus is only on the <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">metacognitive sensitivity</span> of LLMs: the ability of LLMs to distinguish their correct responses from their incorrect ones. Existing studies have already explored the metacognitive sensitivity of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Steyvers (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ackerman (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cash et al. (2025)</a></cite>. However, in almost all of these studies, the metrics used to quantify metacognitive sensitivity present a fundamental limitation. The reason for this is that in order to determine whether an agent has a “good”, “poor”, “optimal”, “sub-optimal”, “better than” or “worse than” metacognitive sensitivity, one must necessarily take into account <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cognitive sensitivity</span> (also known as type 1 sensitivity). Cognitive sensitivity—often quantified by <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>—is the sensitivity associated with the primary task (i.e., the cognitive task targeted by confidence judgments). The key point is that <span id="S1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cognitive sensitivity determines the amount of information available for the subsequent confidence judgment task</span>. This statement can be derived from <span id="S1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">signal detection theory</span> (SDT) under minimal assumptions about how internal evidence is used for confidence judgments.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Having said this, the scope of this statement is not limited to systems strictly following SDT assumptions. We refer to SDT only because it offers a precise mathematical intuition for why cognitive sensitivity determines the amount of information available for the subsequent confidence rating task.</span></span></span> On the one hand, this means that there is an upper bound on metacognitive sensitivity. Optimal metacognitive sensitivity is reached when confidence judgments are made using all the available information.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Having said this, in some specific cases, additional information can be accumulated between the primary task and the confidence judgment task, allowing metacognitive sensitivity to become higher than optimal.</span></span></span> On the other hand, this means that metacognitive sensitivity is highly dependent on cognitive sensitivity. It is crucial to understand what this means. When two agents exhibit different levels of metacognitive sensitivity, this may be nothing more than a direct byproduct of their difference in cognitive sensitivity. In short, without taking cognitive sensitivity—<math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>—into account, one cannot know whether an observed difference in metacognitive sensitivity reflects a genuine difference in metacognitive abilities or merely a difference in cognitive sensitivity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, this problem is well known in the fields of psychology and neuroscience. In these areas, the gold standard metric for metacognitive sensitivity is called meta-<math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fleming (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maniscalco and Lau (2014)</a></cite>, and it has been extensively applied in numerous studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazancieux et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rausch et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carpenter et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conte et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meunier-Duperray et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zakrzewski et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Palmer et al. (2014)</a></cite>. Meta-<math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> has been developed specifically to overcome the problem discussed above. Unlike the other measures of metacognitive sensitivity, meta-<math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> shares the same units as <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Remember that cognitive sensitivity—<math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>—determines the amount of information available for the subsequent confidence judgments task. An <span id="footnote4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ideal observer</span> is able to use all this available information. By definition, meta-<math id="footnote4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="footnote4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> that an <span id="footnote4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ideal observer</span> would need to generate the observed confidence ratings (i.e., the data of the confidence rating task). As we can see, meta-<math id="footnote4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="footnote4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is precisely for this reason that meta-<math id="footnote4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> and the true <math id="footnote4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., the one inferred directly from the data of the cognitive task) share the same unit, both being expressed in <span id="footnote4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">standard deviation</span> units.</span></span></span> As such, meta-<math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> can be compared directly with <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>. To do so, one simply needs to compute the ratio meta-<math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> divided by <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>, known as <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> is a metric of <span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">metacognitive efficiency</span>, which can be defined as <span id="S1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how much of the available information has been used to perform the confidence judgment task</span>. When <math id="S1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> equals 1, this means that all the available information has been used and that metacognitive sensitivity is optimal. On the other hand, when <math id="S1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly lower than 1, this means that only a fraction of the available information has been used and that metacognitive sensitivity is suboptimal. Furthermore, as meta-<math id="S1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> rests on SDT, it remains unaffected by metacognitive bias (at least in principle).<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          The very purpose of SDT is to dissociate <span id="footnote5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sensitivity</span> from <span id="footnote5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bias</span>.</span></span></span> Having said this, the reader should be aware of some limit cases challenging the use of meta-<math id="S1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, the long-held view that <math id="S1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> remains entirely decoupled from cognitive sensitivity and metacognitive bias has recently been challenged <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fleming (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guggenmos (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xue et al. (2021)</a></cite>. Second, since this approach relies on SDT, one must assume that the underlying assumptions of SDT are valid, or at least sufficiently tenable to justify the use of meta-<math id="S1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is why direct extensions of standard SDT have been proposed to relax specific assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miyoshi et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maniscalco and Lau (2014)</a></cite>. Otherwise, it is worth noting that a “model-free” alternative to meta-<math id="S1.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> has recently been developed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dayan (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we argue for the use of the meta-<math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> framework or “model-free” alternatives when assessing the metacognitive sensitivity of AI systems. While recent works have begun to explore this metric in the context of LLMs, its application remains fragmented. Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trinh et al. (2025)</a></cite> applied it to model selection and Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai (2026)</a></cite> investigated the impact of confidence scale design on metacognitive efficiency. Additionally, Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite> utilized a method closely related to meta-<math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> but under an alternative terminology instead of adopting the established one, thereby further obscuring connections between the relevant fields.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">By leveraging the properties of meta-<math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>, we are able to conduct a multi-faceted analysis across three axes of comparison. Note that these comparison axes demonstrate the practical relevance of meta-<math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>, especially when cognitive sensitivity varies. In the following list, we present these axes and discuss the insights they each provide:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compare an LLM to optimality.</span> This is essential to determine whether an LLM’s metacognitive abilities have reached their ceiling or if there remains a significant margin for improvement.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compare different LLMs.</span> When it comes to integrating an LLM into a decision-making process, the question of selecting the most appropriate model always arises. As we have seen, an effective decision-making process must take uncertainty into account (particularly when risk is involved). Therefore, selecting the most appropriate model requires evaluating and comparing different LLMs not only on the cognitive task directly related to the decision-making process but also on their ability to quantify uncertainty. In the current paper, we investigated GPT-5 (reasoning efforts set to “Medium” by default <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI (2026)</a></cite>), DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp (in non-thinking mode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek ()</a></cite>) and Mistral-Medium-2508.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compare the same LLM in the context of different cognitive tasks.</span> This is important because if it turns out that metacognitive efficiency differs significantly depending on the targeted cognitive task, then it becomes difficult, if not impossible, to generalize conclusions regarding the LLM’s metacognitive abilities established in the context of a specific cognitive task. Here, we consider three binary discrimination tasks, labeled A, B and C (see Methods for further details):</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I2.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sentiment analysis task (A).</span> The AI receives a sentence or piece of sentence, whose binary valence (either positive or negative) has been labeled by a human, and it must guess this valence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Oral vs written classification task (B).</span> The AI receives a sentence, that originates from either an oral or written source, and it must guess whether it is oral or written.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Word depletion detection task (C).</span> The AI receives a sentence in which a “the” may have been deleted. The AI has to guess whether a “the” has been removed or if the sentence has been directly submitted unchanged.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the experimental setting at play here is always an LLM conducting a cognitive judgment (type 1 task) followed by a confidence rating—an assessment of the probability that the initial judgment was correct (type 2 task).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, as mentioned above, in a second step, we explore the ability of LLMs to spontaneously adjust their decisions based on <span id="S1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uncertainty</span> and <span id="S1.p13.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">risk configuration</span>. In principle, higher stakes are expected to yield more <span id="S1.p13.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">conservative</span> behavior. For instance, if the cost of failure with respect to a specific choice is catastrophic, then this path should only be considered when uncertainty is near zero. In the context of SDT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Green et al. (1966)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hautus et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wickens (2001)</a></cite>, such sensitivity to uncertainty and risk is captured by the shift of the decision criterion <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The question we ask is the following: depending on the risk configuration, does the LLM calibrate accordingly its type 1 criterion <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>? To answer this question, our experiments are straightforward: the AI performs a type 1 task (we use the same as those presented above) under different risk configurations. Calling S1 and S2 the two possible responses to the type 1 task, we refer to the risk configurations “S1” and “S2” the scenarios in which we use a prompt specifying high risk when answering S1 or S2, respectively, and “None” in case of no specification of risk. As previously, we consider GPT-5, DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp and Mistral-Medium-2508. See Methods for further details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We refer to the experiments in which we estimate meta-<math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> as the “meta-<math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> experiments”, while those focusing on the calibration of <math id="S1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> are termed “<math id="S1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>-calibration experiments” (see Methods for further details).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the large number of trials in our experiments, which can make even negligible effects statistically significant, we distinguish between statistical significance and <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">practical</span> significance by defining <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Regions of Practical Equivalence</span> (ROPEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Makowski et al. (2019)</a></cite>. For more information, see the Methods section. Hence, when a difference is said to be significant without further qualification, it means that it is <span id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">both</span> statistically and practically significant.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Meta-<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> experiments</h3>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results of the meta-<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> experiments are shown in <a href="#S2.F1" title="In Meta-𝑑^′ experiments ‣ 2 Results ‣ Measuring the metacognition of AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and given in <a href="#Sx2.T1" title="In Measuring the metacognition of AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a>. The first, second, and third rows correspond to tasks A, B, and C, respectively. The left column shows <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>, meta-<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> for GPT-5, DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp, and Mistral-Medium-2508, while the right column shows accuracy (proportion of correct responses) as a function of confidence ratings on a five-point scale.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.29693v1/d_and_meta_d.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/374;" width="476" height="374"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><math id="S2.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>, meta-<math id="S2.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> (left column) and the accuracy, or <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29008\delimiter 67273472\text{correct at type 1 task}\mathchar 12906\text{confidence}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28995_{\mathchar 29033}\delimiter 84054785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>correct at type 1 task</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mtext>confidence</mtext><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29008\delimiter 67273472\text{correct at type 1 task}\mathchar 12906\text{confidence}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28995_{\mathchar 29033}\delimiter 84054785</annotation></semantics></math> versus confidence level from C1 to C5 (right column).
The results are shown for task A (top row), task B (middle row) and task C (bottom row).
Error bars in the left column denote 95% CI (see the Methods for details). We tested GPT-5 (blue; left bars), DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp (orange; middle bars) and Mistral-Medium-2508 (yellow; right bars). See the legends on top of panel (a) and in panel (b). When computing accuracy versus confidence (right column), GPT-5 reported confidence C1 in one trial of task B and one trial of task C and similarly, in task B, Mistral-Medium-2508 reported confidence C3 in approximately 0.1% of trials; these observations were excluded, leaving no data points at those confidence levels. The number of trials was <math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28722\mathchar 8706\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28723}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28722\mathchar 8706\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28723}</annotation></semantics></math> for task A and <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28723}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28723}</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise (See the Methods for further details).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In task A, no pairwise differences was observed in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> (the 95% Credible Interval [CI] over the posterior of their difference does not overlap zero; see the Methods section for details). In task B (panel c), DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp shows a significantly larger <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> than those of the other models, which do not differ significantly from one another. Lastly, in task C, GPT-5’s <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> is larger than those of the other models, which do not differ significantly from one another. Notably, in this latter case, the differences in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> between GPT-5 and the other models is considerably more pronounced than in the other two tasks (GPT-5’s type 1 sensitivity is <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28726\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28726\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28723\mathchar 28720\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28723\mathchar 28720\%</annotation></semantics></math> larger than those of DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp and Mistral-Medium-2508, respectively). Regarding differences in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> across tasks for the same model, every model exhibits significant variations across tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to confidence ratings. The right column of <a href="#S2.F1" title="In Meta-𝑑^′ experiments ‣ 2 Results ‣ Measuring the metacognition of AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that, in task A, unlike the other tasks, the models use the full confidence rating scale (see the distributions of confidence ratings in <a href="#Sx2.F1" title="In Measuring the metacognition of AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a>). In particular, in panel (f), we observe that Mistral-Medium-2508 essentially uses only two points of the confidence rating scale, which can occur in cases of poor metacognitive calibration, even in the context of optimal metacognitive sensitivity as discussed in the introduction. In all cases, the models displayed a substantial meta-<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> across tasks, but still suboptimal (the 95% CIs of the posteriors over <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> do not overlap 0 and fall entirely outside the defined ROPE; see the Methods for details).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In task A, the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> of GPT-5 is significantly larger than that of DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28726" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28726</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the difference in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math>). Mistral-Medium-2508’s <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> is larger than that of DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28722" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28722</annotation></semantics></math>) and is not significantly different than that of GPT-5. In task B, GPT-5’s <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> is significantly greater than those of the other two models (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28721" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28721</annotation></semantics></math> for DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp). However, the difference with Mistral-Medium-2508 is practically inconclusive. Mistral-Medium-2508’s <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> is also significantly larger than that of DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp, yet at a practically inconclusive level. In task C, GPT-5 shows an <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> larger than those of the other models. However, these differences are practically inconclusive. The <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> values of DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp and Mistral-Medium-2508 do not differ significantly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Across tasks, the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> values of GPT-5 and DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp do not differ significantly. By contrast, for Mistral-Medium-2805, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> varies significantly across tasks, specifically between task A and task B, and between task A and task C, with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28723" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28723</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28720" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28673\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28720</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">c-calibration experiments</h3>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all models and tasks, we vary the risk configuration (see Introduction and Methods for details). <a href="#S2.F2" title="In c-calibration experiments ‣ 2 Results ‣ Measuring the metacognition of AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the estimated <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> for each scenario, model and task (see also <a href="#Sx2.T2" title="In Measuring the metacognition of AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S2</span></a>). <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29027\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29027\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> is given in <a href="#Sx2.F2" title="In Measuring the metacognition of AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-5 is the only model to exhibit significantly different <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> values across all risk configurations within all tasks. Importantly, these variations in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> values were logically coherent with the changes in risk configuration, as the model’s decision criterion consistently shifted to become more conservative toward the high-risk response (i.e., a shift to the left under the risk configuration “S1” and a shift to the right under the risk configuration “S2”). Notably, under the risk configuration “S1”, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> is always below <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28726" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28726</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> under the risk configuration “S2” is always greater than <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28726" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28726</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For Mistral-Medium-2508 within task B and for DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp within task C, the differences between the risk configurations “None” and “S2” and between the risk configurations “S1” and “None”, respectively, are not significant. Otherwise, all other pairwise comparisons across risk configurations within each tasks are significant and consistent with the specified risk configuration. However, when compared to the defined ROPE (see Methods for details), the differences in task A between the risk configurations “S1” and “None” and between the risk configurations “S2” and “None” for DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp are practically inconclusive. Similarly, in task C, the difference between the risk configurations “S1” and “None” for Mistral-Medium-2508 is inconclusive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that several variations in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> between risk configurations were found to be significant. Specifically, this is the case for GPT-5 within task B between the risk configurations “None” and “S2” (relative difference of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28728\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28728\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and within task C between the risk configurations “None” and “S1” (relative difference of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28724\mathchar 28721\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28724\mathchar 28721\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and between the risk configurations “S2” and “S1’ (relative difference of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28723\mathchar 28727\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28723\mathchar 28727\%</annotation></semantics></math>). Within task B, DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp also exhibits a significant change in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> between “S1” and “S2” (relative difference of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28723\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28723\%</annotation></semantics></math>). In contrast, all other differences were either non-significant (19 cases) or, while statistically significant, proved to be practically inconclusive (4 cases).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">When differences in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> are significant, the normalized criterion <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29027\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29027\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferable to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>, as it accounts for the magnitude of the bias <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relative to the sensitivity</span>. We therefore examine the differences in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> for these specific cases. Notably, the comparisons of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> mirror those of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These results show that, in most cases across tasks and models, we were able to induce a conservative behavior toward either S1 or S2 by specifying a risk configuration in natural language through prompt design. In other words this demonstrates a general ability of LLMs to calibrate their type 1 criterion in a context-dependent manner.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.29693v1/regulation_of_c.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/329;" width="476" height="329"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Type 1 criterion <math id="S2.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> across three risk configurations (“S1”, “None” , “S2”) within task A (top row), task B (middle row) and task C (bottom row), for GPT-5 (left column), DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp (middle column), and Mistral-Medium-2508 (right column). Error bars represent 95% confidence intervals estimated via the Delta method. Although the Delta method can be sensitive to extreme proportions (that is, hit rates or false alarm rates close to 0 or 1), as may occur when responses are strongly biased toward S1 or S2, the large trial count (<math id="S2.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29006\mathchar 12821\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28724}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29006\mathchar 12821\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28724}</annotation></semantics></math>) mitigates potential issues. See the Methods for details.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we argued for the use of the meta-<math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> framework or “model-free” alternatives when assessing the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">metacognitive sensitivity</span> of AI systems. Moreover, we also proposed to leverage SDT to measure the ability of AIs to spontaneously regulate their decisions based on uncertainty and risk. We showcased these psychophysical approaches by conducting two series of experiments on LLMs. In the first, our multi-faceted application of meta-<math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> allowed us to compare LLMs to optimality, compare different LLMs on the same task, and compare the same LLM across different tasks. Notably, our results reveal that LLMs exhibit measurable metacognitive sensitivity. More precisely, all three models tested—GPT-5, DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp, and Mistral-Medium-2508—exhibited substantial values of meta-<math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that their confidence judgments meaningfully tracked their type 1 performance, with variable <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> levels, ranging from moderate (<math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 12824\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28726\mathchar 28725" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 12824\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28726\mathchar 28725</annotation></semantics></math>) to near-optimal (<math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 12824\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28729\mathchar 28720" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 12824\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28729\mathchar 28720</annotation></semantics></math>). Notably, we found some variability in <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> across tasks (for the same model) and across models (for the same task). The second experiments, focused on shifts in <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>, highlighted the remarkable ability of LLMs to become more conservative in their decision-making in a way consistent with the specified risk.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that many aspects of our experiments can be tuned in a way that may affect the results. For example, instead of submitting the sentences one by one, we could submit them all at once, or in batches, to see whether this affects their responses as having access to all sentences could potentially impact their cognitive and metacognitive calibration. Similarly, for each trial, we could ask them to perform the type 1 task and the type 2 task in two successive prompts rather than in the same prompt. Also, it has been reported in Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite> that varying the design of confidence scale, as changing the number of confidence levels (e.g., <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delimiter 67482370\mathchar 28720\mathchar 24891\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720\delimiter 84267779" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delimiter 67482370\mathchar 28720\mathchar 24891\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720\delimiter 84267779</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delimiter 67482370\mathchar 28720\mathchar 24891\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28720\delimiter 84267779" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delimiter 67482370\mathchar 28720\mathchar 24891\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28720\delimiter 84267779</annotation></semantics></math>) affects metacognitive efficiency. One can also tune models’ parameters such as the temperature or the level of “reasoning efforts”. Finally, remember that our article is not a benchmark paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We applied our approach in the context of simple binary discrimination tasks. However, it should be noted that the challenge of evaluating AI metacognition requires not only robust metrics but also the development of adequate tasks—specifically, tasks that actively engage the targeted metacognitive skills. For example, to evaluate the metacognitive competencies at stake in the “AI decision-making” scenario, we advocate for the use of experimental paradigms where the model must manage the exploration-exploitation trade-off (a dimension not addressed in our experiments). A compelling example of such a task is the <span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Eleusis Benchmark</span> recently proposed by David Louapre in Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Louapre (2026)</a></cite>. In this task, the agent must find a hidden rule behind a sequence of cards. To do so, it must decide at each step whether to sample more evidence (play a new card and receive feedback) or to commit to an answer. Crucially, the more delayed the successful guess and the more wrong answers, the less points. This creates a critical tension: responding too early leads to errors due to uncertainty, while responding too late costs points. While Louapre identified distinct “player profiles”—ranging from bold to cautious or intermediate—our research adds a crucial layer to this observation: we have demonstrated that a single LLM is capable of strategic flexibility. Rather than being locked into one profile, these models can shift their behavior when the cost of error and context change. Finally, integrating such tasks with our psychophysical analysis would allow for a much finer characterization of AI metacognition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to the well-known issue of hallucinations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alansari and Luqman (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hicks et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Hallucinations are typically framed as factual errors communicated as <span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">undisputed</span> facts. Hence, hallucinations are not merely “cognitive errors”; they also involve a “metacognitive error,” in that the model hallucinates a high confidence level in spite of uncertainty. The present article does not address this pathological regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, while this study has focused on the ability of AI to estimate (and report) its own confidence, another crucial skill in some collaborative decision-making scenarios is the capacity to integrate confidence signals communicated by other agents—whether human or artificial. Future experiments will consist in providing an LLM with a recommendation accompanied by a confidence score whenever it is about to perform a type 1 task. The question we ask is: does the extent to which the AI takes into account the recommendation depends on the communicated confidence? Such experiments are crucial for evaluating whether AI systems can behave meaningfully when participating in collaborative decision-making processes. Note that a gap in the interpretation of confidence signals may arise between collaborating agents depending on how this information is formulated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steyvers et al. (2025b)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, our work highlights the relevance of the meta-<math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> and SDT approaches in the study of AI metacognition. Crucially, the growing integration of AI into decision-making workflows makes the characterization of AI metacognition an increasingly pressing need.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methods</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tasks and datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In all experiments, the models are asked to perform a two-alternative forced-choice discrimination task (either the sentiment analysis task [A], oral vs written classification task [B], or word depletion detection task [C]; see below). In the meta-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> experiments, they are also asked to rate their confidence on a scale from 1 to 5. The prompts are available on the GitHub page given below. Prompts for the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>-calibration experiments may specify a risk configuration (see the Introduction for explanations). In all cases, we submitted one input (e.g., a sentence to discriminate) at a time, each submission effectively corresponding to a new conversation. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sentiment analysis task (A).</span> The AI receives a sentence or piece of sentence, whose binary valence (either positive or negative) has been labeled by a human, and it must guess this valence. We used the SST-2 dataset freely available at <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/stanfordnlp/sst2" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/stanfordnlp/sst2</a>. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Oral vs written classification task (B).</span> The AI receives a sentence, that originates from either an oral or written source, and it must guess whether it is oral or written. We used a labeled dataset freely available at <a href="https://zenodo.org/records/7694423" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://zenodo.org/records/7694423</a>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Word depletion detection task (C).</span> Using the same dataset from task B, we selected sentences comprising at least one “the”, and deleted one with a probability of 0.5. The AI has to guess whether a “the” has been removed or if the sentence has been directly submitted unchanged.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We give a succinct introduction to type 1 sensitivity (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>), type 1 criterion (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>), and metacognitive sensitivity (meta-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>) (see Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maniscalco and Lau (2014)</a></cite> for a thorough derivation). Suppose that a subject performs repeated trials of a discrimination task in which the only possible responses are S1 and S2. Given a stimulus, which is either S1 or S2, consider an internal response evaluated along a one-dimensional axis <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29048</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us define <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29030\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29048\mathchar 12906\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28721\delimiter 84054785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29030\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29048\mathchar 12906\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28721\delimiter 84054785</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29030\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29048\mathchar 12906\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28722\delimiter 84054785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29030\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29048\mathchar 12906\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28722\delimiter 84054785</annotation></semantics></math> to be normal distributions with equal unit variance. <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> is then defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29050\delimiter 67273472\text{HR}\delimiter 84054785\mathchar 8704\mathchar 29050\delimiter 67273472\text{FAR}\delimiter 84054785" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>HR</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>FAR</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29050\delimiter 67273472\text{HR}\delimiter 84054785\mathchar 8704\mathchar 29050\delimiter 67273472\text{FAR}\delimiter 84054785</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29050" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29050</annotation></semantics></math> is the inverse of the normal cumulative distribution function, HR is the hit rate (proportion of successful judgments when S2 was presented), and FAR is the false-alarm rate (proportion of incorrect judgments when S1 was presented).
Notably, <a href="#S4.E1" title="In Metrics ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Measuring the metacognition of AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reduces to</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28950_{\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28722}\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28950_{\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28721}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28950_{\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28722}\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28950_{\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28721}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28950_{\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28721}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28950_{\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28721}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28950_{\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28722}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28950_{\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28722}</annotation></semantics></math> are the means of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29030\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29048\mathchar 12906\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28721\delimiter 84054785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29030\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29048\mathchar 12906\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28721\delimiter 84054785</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29030\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29048\mathchar 12906\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28722\delimiter 84054785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29030\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29048\mathchar 12906\mathchar 29011\mathchar 28722\delimiter 84054785</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the extent to which the subject is able to discriminate the two types of stimuli—S1 and S2. Consider a decision criterion <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> which is the value of the internal response <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29048</annotation></semantics></math> below and above which the subject responds S1 and S2, respectively. It is possible to write <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of HR and FAR:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027\mathchar 12349\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28725\mathchar 8706\left\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29050\delimiter 67273472\text{HR}\delimiter 84054785\mathchar 8235\mathchar 29050\delimiter 67273472\text{FAR}\delimiter 84054785\right\delimiter 84054785\mathchar 314" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>HR</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>FAR</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027\mathchar 12349\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28725\mathchar 8706\left\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 29050\delimiter 67273472\text{HR}\delimiter 84054785\mathchar 8235\mathchar 29050\delimiter 67273472\text{FAR}\delimiter 84054785\right\delimiter 84054785\mathchar 314</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The smaller <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>, the stronger the internal response required for the subject to respond S1; that is, greater evidence is needed for a S1 response. We also define <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29027\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29027\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">After each trial, the subject is asked to rate its confidence on a given scale, from 1 to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29000</annotation></semantics></math>. Meta-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> that would be expected given confidence ratings only, if they were generated by an ideal observer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fleming (2017)</a></cite>. More generally, let us define <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28946\mathchar 12349\left\delimiter 67273472\text{meta-}\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 24891\text{meta-}\mathchar 29027\mathchar 24891\text{meta-}\textbf{{\lx@text@underline{c}}}_{\mathchar 28722}\right\delimiter 84054785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>meta-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>meta-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mtext>meta-</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic">c</mtext></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28946\mathchar 12349\left\delimiter 67273472\text{meta-}\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 24891\text{meta-}\mathchar 29027\mathchar 24891\text{meta-}\textbf{{\lx@text@underline{c}}}_{\mathchar 28722}\right\delimiter 84054785</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{{\lx@text@underline{c}}}_{\mathchar 28722}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic ltx_underline">c</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{{\lx@text@underline{c}}}_{\mathchar 28722}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the type 2 criteria <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maniscalco and Lau (2014)</a></cite>. The estimation of meta-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> is typically implemented using a maximum likelihood estimation:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathchar 28946^{\mathchar 8707}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>UNKNOWN</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathchar 28946^{\mathchar 8707}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathchar 12349\underset{\mathchar 28946}{\text{arg~max }}\mathchar 29004\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 28946\mathchar 12906\text{confidence ratings}\delimiter 84054785\text{, subject to }\text{meta-}\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 24891\,\mathchar 28941\left\delimiter 67273472\text{meta-}\textbf{{\lx@text@underline{c}}}_{\text{ascending}}\right\delimiter 84054785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>arg max </mtext><mo>UNDERACCENT</mo></munder><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>UNKNOWN</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext>confidence ratings</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext>, subject to </mtext><mtext>meta-</mtext></mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>
</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mtext>meta-</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic">c</mtext></mrow><mtext>ascending</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathchar 12349\underset{\mathchar 28946}{\text{arg~max }}\mathchar 29004\delimiter 67273472\mathchar 28946\mathchar 12906\text{confidence ratings}\delimiter 84054785\text{, subject to }\text{meta-}\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 29027^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 24891\,\mathchar 28941\left\delimiter 67273472\text{meta-}\textbf{{\lx@text@underline{c}}}_{\text{ascending}}\right\delimiter 84054785</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29004" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29004</annotation></semantics></math> is the log-likelihood and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28941\left\delimiter 67273472\text{meta-}\textbf{{\lx@text@underline{c}}}_{\text{ascending}}\right\delimiter 84054785" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mtext>meta-</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic">c</mtext></mrow><mtext>ascending</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28941\left\delimiter 67273472\text{meta-}\textbf{{\lx@text@underline{c}}}_{\text{ascending}}\right\delimiter 84054785</annotation></semantics></math> is a Boolean function that returns true only if the type 1 and type 2 criteria are ordered appropriately. Metacognitive efficiency is defined as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\text{meta-}\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>meta-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}\mathchar 12349\text{meta-}\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is possible to compute meta-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> using a hierarchical Bayesian model that outputs a full <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">posterior</span>. This approach offers more reliability with sparser data, among other advantages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fleming (2017)</a></cite>. In the current work, we used the implementation of this approach provided in Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fleming (2017)</a></cite>, available at <a href="https://github.com/smfleming/HMeta-d" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/smfleming/HMeta-d</a>. Yet, in the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>-calibration experiments, we directly applied the formula of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> from type 1 response counts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Number of trials.</h3>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For task A, the measured <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> were significantly large (above 3). Consequently, we submitted <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28722\mathchar 8706\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28724}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28722\mathchar 8706\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28724}</annotation></semantics></math> sentences for each estimation. This trial count was informed by tests with simulated data (see <a href="#Sx2.F3" title="In Measuring the metacognition of AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S3</span></a>). For all other tasks, the trial count was set to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28724}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28720^{\mathchar 28724}</annotation></semantics></math>. These large trial counts also support the use of the Delta method to quantify uncertainty in the estimates.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Statistical analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fleming (2017)</a></cite>, to assess statistical significance of differences between values of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> and values of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> in the meta-<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> experiments, we checked whether the <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">symmetric 95% credible interval</span> (CI) of the posterior distribution of their log ratio overlap zero using Fleming’s hierarchical Bayesian model and specifically the function <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fit_meta_d_mcmc.m</span> from Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fleming (2017)</a></cite> and freely available on the GitHub page associated with the paper, where we set <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mcmc_params.estimate_dprime = 1</span>. To assess the optimality of the models’ metacognition, we checked whether the CI of the posterior distribution of the log <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29005_{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}</annotation></semantics></math> overlap zero. To assess the statistical significance of differences in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>-calibration experiments, we checked whether <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delimiter 69640972\mathchar 29018\delimiter 69640972\mathchar 12821\mathchar 28723\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28721" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delimiter 69640972\mathchar 29018\delimiter 69640972\mathchar 12821\mathchar 28723\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\mathchar 28721</annotation></semantics></math>, where we applied a Bonferroni correction <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 28939^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28720\mathchar 28725\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29037" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 28939^{\mathchar 560}\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28720\mathchar 28725\delimiter 68408078\mathchar 29037</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29037\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28727" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29037\mathchar 12349\mathchar 28722\mathchar 28727</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise comparisons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dunn (1961)</a></cite>, for a two-sided Z-test where we computed the variances of the differences using the Delta method. Given the large number of trials in our experiments, which can make even negligible effects statistically significant, we defined Regions of Practical Equivalence (ROPE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Makowski et al. (2019)</a></cite>. Hence, to assess the <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">practical</span> significance of differences in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\mathchar 29005_{\mathrm{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\mathchar 29005_{\mathrm{\mathchar 29042\mathchar 29025\mathchar 29044\mathchar 29033\mathchar 29039}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use a ROPE of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delimiter 67482370\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28720\mathchar 28725\mathchar 24891\,\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28720\mathchar 28725\delimiter 84267779" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delimiter 67482370\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28720\mathchar 28725\mathchar 24891\,\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28720\mathchar 28725\delimiter 84267779</annotation></semantics></math>. For differences in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29028^{\mathchar 560}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathchar 29027" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathchar 29027</annotation></semantics></math>, we consider a ROPE of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delimiter 67482370\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\mathchar 24891\,\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\delimiter 84267779" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delimiter 67482370\mathchar 8704\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\mathchar 24891\,\mathchar 28720\mathchar 314\mathchar 28721\delimiter 84267779</annotation></semantics></math>. In each case, we considered differences <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">practically</span> significant if the 95% CI fell entirely outside the ROPE, and on the contrary negligible if entirely inside and inconclusive in the case of an overlap.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Code and software availability</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our scripts and prompts are freely available at</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="https://github.com/sshrichard/metacognition-of-AI" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/sshrichard/metacognition-of-AI</a></p>
</div>
</section>
